---
import Layout from "@/components/Layout.astro";
import { meta } from "@/lib/load";
import { formatDate } from "@/lib/format";
import { withBase } from "@/lib/paths";

const c = meta.counts;
const corpusStart = meta.corpusStart ? formatDate(meta.corpusStart) : "11 November 2025";
---

<Layout title="About">
  <div class="page about prose stack">
    <h1>About this tracker</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Under the <a href="https://www.digital.gov.au/ai/ai-in-government-policy"
        >Policy for the responsible use of AI in government</a
      >, non-corporate Commonwealth entities have had to <a
        href="https://www.digital.gov.au/ai/ai-in-government-policy/standard-ai-transparency-statements"
        >publish AI transparency statements since February 2025</a
      >. This project keeps a dated copy of every version of those statements and
      shows what changed between them.
    </p>

    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <p>
      Every day a scraper visits each agency&rsquo;s statement, saves a copy, and
      records anything that has changed since the previous day. The result is a
      dated history of every statement, down to the wording. Apart from the
      analysis described below, the site adds no commentary of its own.
    </p>

    <h2>Following changes</h2>
    <p>
      When an agency publishes or revises its statement, the tracker announces it
      from <a href="https://bsky.app/profile/apsaitracker.app"
        >@apsaitracker.app</a
      > on Bluesky. Follow that account to hear about changes as they happen.
    </p>
    <p>
      The whole dataset is also published as machine-readable records on the AT
      Protocol, the open network Bluesky runs on. The <a href={withBase("/data/")}
        >data downloads</a
      > page covers that mirror and the other ways to get the data.
    </p>

    <h2>Origins</h2>
    <p>
      This tracker began in the audience. While the Australian Public
      Service&rsquo;s <a
        href="https://ministers.finance.gov.au/financeminister/speech/2025/11/12/launch-ai-plan-australian-public-service"
        >AI Plan</a
      > was being launched in November 2025, Ben Swift opened a laptop and started
      archiving the agencies&rsquo; transparency statements. The record therefore
      opens on {corpusStart}, and the statements&rsquo; first nine months fall
      outside it; <a href={withBase("/reading#corpus")}>how to read the data</a>
      covers what that means for the figures.
    </p>

    <h2>Methodology</h2>
    <p>
      <a href={withBase("/reading")}>How to read the data</a> is the plain-language
      guide to the scores and colours on this site, including what they
      don&rsquo;t claim. The notes below give the technical method.
    </p>

    <h3>Who we track</h3>
    <p>
      The list is checked against two sources. The Department of Finance&rsquo;s <a
        href="https://www.finance.gov.au/government/managing-commonwealth-resources/structure-australian-government-public-sector/pgpa-act-flipchart-and-list"
        >list of Commonwealth entities</a
      > under the PGPA Act says which bodies are non-corporate entities and so bound
      by the policy; the DTA&rsquo;s <a
        href="https://www.digital.gov.au/policy/ai/list-of-transparency-statements"
        >central register</a
      > records who has published. Every non-corporate entity is listed here
      whether or not it has published. Corporate entities and other bodies are
      added once we find a statement of theirs, and a handful that have never
      published stay on the list to show the gap. The <a
        href={withBase("/agencies")}>agencies page</a
      > sets out which bodies are bound, which are only encouraged, and which are
      exempt.
    </p>

    <h3>Changes</h3>
    <p>
      The <a href={withBase("/reading#anatomy")}>anatomy of a change</a> explains
      how each change is read; this is the machinery underneath. Changes come
      straight from git, and a scrape glitch that is later reverted collapses out.
      Captures known to have failed are listed in the repository
      (<code>captures.toml</code>) and dropped before anything reads them, as are
      captures the model judges not to be the statement. A new capture that has
      lost more than half its text is held back until someone confirms it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Claude Opus 5.5 does the reading, and every summary names its model. Capture
      checks, change classifications and profiles are cached by content hash in the
      repository, so each is made once and the site rebuilds without model calls.
    </p>
    <p>
      A statement&rsquo;s first appearance is labelled &ldquo;first tracked&rdquo;
      (the day-one bulk import) or &ldquo;added&rdquo; (an agency we began tracking
      later). It is never labelled &ldquo;published&rdquo;, because we know only
      when a statement entered the tracker, and the agency&rsquo;s actual
      publication date may be earlier.
    </p>

    <h3>Propagation</h3>
    <p>
      Shared passages are found lexically. Passages are normalised (links, markup,
      punctuation and case removed) and matched across agencies, with a separate
      pass for canonical template phrases. The site claims only that a passage
      appears in more than one statement and says nothing about who copied whom,
      though passages that also appear in the DTA&rsquo;s own statement are marked.
    </p>

    <h3>Profiles</h3>
    <p>
      A profile is JSON validated against a closed vocabulary for each answer. A
      revision a rule marks as noise, such as a ticking date stamp, keeps the
      previous revision&rsquo;s profile; every other revision is read, with the
      previous profile as its baseline. The scraper records a page&rsquo;s own
      last-updated date and strips that line, so a changing date stamp
      doesn&rsquo;t show up in the diffs, and if the page has no such line the
      model looks for a date in the text. Commitments are matched across revisions
      by word overlap.
    </p>

    <h2>Related work</h2>
    <p>
      Other researchers study these statements, but as far as we know nobody else
      tracks how they <em>change</em>. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.26075"
        >Pan et al. (2026)</a
      >, who assembled the <em>AITS-101</em> dataset for the first systematic
      analysis of the Australian corpus, and an <a
        href="https://www.admscentre.org.au/government-ai-transparency-statements-hard-to-find-new-report-finds/"
        >audit by the ADM+S Centre</a
      > both treat the statements as a single snapshot. The Digital Transformation
      Agency&rsquo;s <a
        href="https://www.dta.gov.au/articles/new-central-register-ai-transparency-statements-commonwealth-entities"
        >central register</a
      > aims to surface major updates but publishes no version history.
    </p>
    <p>
      The method is borrowed. Longitudinal corpora of privacy policies, such as the <a
        href="https://privacypolicies.cs.princeton.edu/">Princeton&ndash;Leuven
        corpus</a
      >, and terms-of-service trackers have long archived each snapshot and diffed
      it; this project does the same for government AI transparency statements.
      The nearest efforts abroad either register AI <em>use</em> itself, like the
      UK&rsquo;s <a
        href="https://www.gov.uk/government/collections/algorithmic-transparency-recording-standard-hub"
        >Algorithmic Transparency Recording Standard</a
      > and the Amsterdam and Helsinki algorithm registers, or score transparency
      over time, like Stanford&rsquo;s <a
        href="https://news.stanford.edu/stories/2025/12/foundation-model-transparency-index-ai-companies-information"
        >Foundation Model Transparency Index</a
      >. None of them diffs the text of agency statements.
    </p>

    <h2>Caveats</h2>
    <p>
      Scraping is imperfect: site redesigns, bot blocks and PDF extraction all
      introduce noise. A body counts as not having published when we have searched
      and found nothing, and that is weaker evidence than a find, since a statement
      filed somewhere unusual or behind a bot block can be missed. Treat the figures
      as indicative, and follow the source link on any statement to check the
      original.
    </p>

    <h2>Credits</h2>
    <p>
      This tracker is a <a
        href="https://cybernetics.anu.edu.au/cybernetic-studio/"
        >Cybernetic Studio</a
      > project created by <a href="https://benswift.me">Dr Ben Swift</a> at the <a
        href="https://cybernetics.anu.edu.au">ANU School of Cybernetics</a
      >.
    </p>

    <h2>Citation and referencing</h2>
    <p>
      This tracker is archived on Zenodo with a permanent DOI, and Ben Swift is its
      sole creator and maintainer. If you use the project, its data or its
      analysis, please cite or credit it; that also helps others find the source
      and check it for themselves.
    </p>
    <p>
      The DOI <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20842437"
        ><code>10.5281/zenodo.20842437</code></a
      > is a <em>concept</em> DOI: it always resolves to the latest archived
      version, so a citation stays current as new versions are released.
    </p>
    <p>In scholarly work, cite it as:</p>
    <blockquote class="about__cite">
      Swift, B. (2025). <cite>Australian Government AI Tracker</cite>
      [Software]. ANU School of Cybernetics. <a
        href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20842437"
        >https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20842437</a
      >
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      A machine-readable <a
        href="https://github.com/ANUcybernetics/aps-ai-tracker/blob/main/CITATION.cff"
        ><code>CITATION.cff</code></a
      > in the repository can generate the equivalent BibTeX or other formats, and
      the Zenodo record exports them directly.
    </p>
    <p>
      In a newspaper, blog or other non-academic piece, a credit with a link is
      enough, for example:
    </p>
    <blockquote class="about__cite">
      Data from the <a href={withBase("/")}>Australian Government AI Tracker</a>,
      a project by Dr Ben Swift at the ANU School of Cybernetics.
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      Please link to the site as well as naming it, so readers can reach the
      underlying record. When you quote a figure, include the date you read it,
      because the corpus is updated daily.
    </p>

    <h2>Licensing</h2>
    <p>
      The scraper and this site are released under the MIT License. The dataset
      (the <a href={withBase("/data/")}>downloads</a>, including the derived fields
      in them) is licensed{" "}
      <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>. The
      statement text belongs to the respective agencies; most Australian Government
      content is licensed CC BY 4.0, but check each agency&rsquo;s site for specifics.
    </p>

    <p class="muted about__build">
      {c.statements} statements, {c.changes} changes of substance across {c.revisions}{" "}
      captures, {c.profiled} profiled. Built {formatDate(meta.builtAt)} from commit{" "}
      <a href={`https://github.com/ANUcybernetics/aps-ai-tracker/commit/${meta.headSha}`}
        >{meta.headSha.slice(0, 7)}</a
      >.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .about {
    max-width: 46rem;
  }

  .about__cite {
    margin-block: var(--space-3);
    padding-inline-start: var(--space-3);
    border-inline-start: 1px solid var(--border);
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .about__cite cite {
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .about__build {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-6);
    padding-block-start: var(--space-3);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }
</style>
